  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Linil Kumar.C.R vs Food Safety Officer on 1 March, 2016 Author: V Raja Vijayaraghavan Bench: V Raja Vijayaraghavan In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present: The Honourable Mr. Justice Raja Vijayaraghavan V Wednesday, The 23Rd Day Of November 2016/2Nd Agrahayana, 1938 Crl.MC.No. 1735 of 2016 ----------------------- Cc 3605/2015 Of Judicial First Class Magistrate Court, Thripunithura ----------- Petitioner(S)/Accused No.2: -------------------------- Linil Kumar.C.R, Red Chillies, Xvi/617, 622, Near Lakeshoare Hospital, Nettoor, Maradu, Ernakulam(Licensee) (Address As Shown In The Complaint). By Adv. Sri.N.A.Muraleedharan Respondent(S)/Complaint/State: ------------------------------ 1. Food Safety Officer, Thripunithura Circle-682 301. 2. State Of Kerala, Represented By The Public Prosecutor, High Court Of Kerala, Ernakulam-682 031. By Public Prosecutor Sri.M.R.Dhanil",
    "facts": "The petitioner is the 2nd accused in C.C.No.3605 of 2015 on the file of the Judicial Magistrate of First Class, Thripunithura. 2.The aforesaid case arose out of a complaint filed by the Food Safety Officer alleging infringement of Section 3(1) (zz)(v) & (vii), Section 26(2)(i) , Section 27(1), Section 38(1) and Section 59(iii) of the Food Safety and Standards Act, 2006 (hereinafter referred to as 'the FSS Act, 2006'), read with Regulation 2.12 of the Food Safety and Standards (Food Product Standards and Food Additives) Regulations, 2011 (hereinafter referred to as \"the Regulations, 2011\") . 3.The allegation in the complaint is that the sample of `Fish Fry' purchased for analysis on 19.11.2014 by the 1st respondent from the restaurant \"Red Chilies\", of which the 1st accused is the food business operator, was reported to be unsafe on analysis by the public analyst as it contained sunset yellow, a synthetic colour, contravening the provisions of the FSS Act, 2006 and the Regulations framed therefrom. 4.The aforesaid proceedings are sought to be [LEAKAGE_MASK] invoking the powers of this Court under Section 482 of the Code of Criminal procedure.",
    "arguments": "6.It is submitted by the learned counsel appearing for the petitioner that the petitioner has been arrayed on the sole ground that he was the licencee of the restaurant. Referring to Annexure-I, it is contended that though on 07/09/2012, license under the FSS Act, 2006 was taken in the name of the petitioner it was valid only till 31/03/2013. To bring home this point, reliance is placed on Annexure-III communication issued by the Assistant Commissioner of Food Safety, Ernakulam which reveals that the validity of the license issued in the name of the petitioner was from 01/04/2012 to 31/03/2013. Elaborating further, it is urged by the learned Counsel that the 1st respondent has also not issued any notice to the petitioner calling upon him to exercise his option to prefer an appeal against the report of the food analyst before the designated officer. The learned counsel drew the attention of this Court to Annexure-V copy of acknowledgment, which reveals that the statutory notice was accepted by the 1st accused for and on behalf of \"Red Chillies\". Finally, it is submitted that the sanction was accorded by the Commissioner of Food Safety to prosecute the petitioner by impressing upon the said authority that the statutory notice was served on the petitioner and that he had failed to prefer an appeal under Section 46(4) of the FSS Act, 2006. 7.The learned Public Prosecutor on the other hand refuted the submissions and contended that the petitioner is none other than the brother-in-law of the 1st accused. Though the petitioner was not in station at the relevant point of time, the licence was initially obtained in his name. No prejudice has been caused to the petitioner and the grounds raised by the petitioner is not sufficient to quash the proceedings, is the submission.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "6.It is submitted by the learned counsel appearing for the petitioner that the petitioner has been arrayed on the sole ground that he was the licencee of the restaurant. Referring to Annexure-I, it is contended that though on 07/09/2012, license under the FSS Act, 2006 was taken in the name of the petitioner it was valid only till 31/03/2013. To bring home this point, reliance is placed on Annexure-III communication issued by the Assistant Commissioner of Food Safety, Ernakulam which reveals that the validity of the license issued in the name of the petitioner was from 01/04/2012 to 31/03/2013. Elaborating further, it is urged by the learned Counsel that the 1st respondent has also not issued any notice to the petitioner calling upon him to exercise his option to prefer an appeal against the report of the food analyst before the designated officer. The learned counsel drew the attention of this Court to Annexure-V copy of acknowledgment, which reveals that the statutory notice was accepted by the 1st accused for and on behalf of \"Red Chillies\". Finally, it is submitted that the sanction was accorded by the Commissioner of Food Safety to prosecute the petitioner by impressing upon the said authority that the statutory notice was served on the petitioner and that he had failed to prefer an appeal under Section 46(4) of the FSS Act, 2006.",
    "respondent_arguments": "7.The learned Public Prosecutor on the other hand refuted the submissions and contended that the petitioner is none other than the brother-in-law of the 1st accused. Though the petitioner was not in station at the relevant point of time, the licence was initially obtained in his name. No prejudice has been caused to the petitioner and the grounds raised by the petitioner is not sufficient to quash the proceedings, is the submission.",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": ""
  },
